  "theory": {
        "intro": "An identity about binomial coefficients can be proved by substituting a chosen value for $x$ in the expansion $(1+x)^{n} = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} {^{n}C_{r}} x^{r}$. The value you choose decides which identity comes out.",
        "facts": [
          "Substituting $x = 1$ gives the total ${^{n}C_{0}} + {^{n}C_{1}} + {^{n}C_{2}} + \\dots + {^{n}C_{n}} = 2^{n}$.",
          "Substituting $x = -1$ gives the alternating sum ${^{n}C_{0}} - {^{n}C_{1}} + {^{n}C_{2}} - \\dots + (-1)^{n} {^{n}C_{n}} = 0$.",
          "Adding those two results gives $\\sum_{\\text{even } r} {^{n}C_{r}} = 2^{n-1}$, and subtracting them gives $\\sum_{\\text{odd } r} {^{n}C_{r}} = 2^{n-1}$.",
          "Substituting $x = k$ gives ${^{n}C_{0}} + k {^{n}C_{1}} + k^{2} {^{n}C_{2}} + \\dots + k^{n} {^{n}C_{n}} = (1+k)^{n}$.",
          "In every proof, state the expansion you start from and the exact value you substitute."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "State the general binomial expansion $(1+x)^{n} = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} {^{n}C_{r}} x^{r}$ or $(a+b)^{n} = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} {^{n}C_{r}} a^{n-r} b^{r}$",
          "Substitute the chosen value(s) for the variable(s) into both the expanded form and the factored form",
          "Simplify the resulting arithmetic expressions or combine equations by addition or subtraction to establish the identity"
        ]
      },
